-- title:
--   Steinberg U_q lies in the image of Ψ
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, let $p$ be a prime with $p\neq 2$ and $p\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k$: a $k$-vector space $V$ of dimension $2$ together with a monoid homomorphism $\bar\rho.\rho$ from $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to $\mathrm{End}_k V$ trivial on the elements fixing some finite subextension of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$, assumed absolutely irreducible, i.e. the base change $\overline{k}\otimes_k V$ has no Galois-stable submodule other than $\bot$ and $\top$. Let $S_0\subseteq S$ be finite sets of primes with $p\in S$, and $N\geq 1$ an integer all of whose prime factors lie in $S$ and none in $S_0$, with: $p^2\nmid N$; $\bar\rho$ unramified at every $q\in S$, $q\neq p$, $q\nmid N$ (unramified meaning $\bar\rho(\sigma)=1$ for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of every valuation subring $P\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q\in P.\mathrm{nonunits}$); for every prime $q\neq p$ with $q\,\|\,N$, $\bar\rho$ ramified at $q$ and unipotent on inertia at $q$ (characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$ on all inertia elements); for every prime $q\neq p$ with $q^2\mid N$, $q^3\nmid N$ and unipotence on inertia at $q$; and, if $p\mid N$, ordinarity at $p$ (a line spanned by a basis vector, stable under the decomposition group, containing $(\bar\rho(\sigma)-1)V$ for inertia $\sigma$). Assume weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ are $\mathbb C$-spanned by those with integral $q$-expansions. Let $\theta'$ be a ring homomorphism to $k$ from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S₀`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S_0$, and the $U_q$ for primes $q\mid N$, $q\notin S_0$, subject to: the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ equals $X^2-\theta'(T_\ell)X+\ell$ for every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $\ell$ (a decomposition-group element acting as $x\mapsto x^\ell$ on the residue field of $P$) whenever $\ell\neq p$, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S_0$; $\theta'(U_q)=0$ when $q^2\mid N$; $\theta'(U_q)$ equals the trace of the Frobenius-induced endomorphism of the inertia coinvariants $V/\sum_{\tau}\mathrm{im}(\bar\rho(\tau)-1)$ when $q\,\|\,N$; and $\theta'(T_p)$ likewise if $p\nmid N$, $p\notin S_0$. Let $\theta$ factor through $\theta'$ along the inclusion [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) $\subseteq$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S₀`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), and let $\Psi$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra map from [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) to [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S₀ 𝒪 θ'`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) (localisations of the base-changed Hecke lattice algebras at the complements of the primes cut out by $\theta$, $\theta'$) commuting with the canonical maps [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151) along that inclusion. Then for a prime $q\neq p$ with $q\mid N$, $q^2\nmid N$, at which $\bar\rho$ is ramified and unipotent on inertia, and with $\theta'(U_q)$ given by the Frobenius trace on inertia coinvariants at $q$, there exists $u$ in [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) with $\Psi(u)=\pi(U_q)$.
--
--   This is the statement that the operator $U_q$ at a Steinberg prime $q\,\|\,N$, $q\neq p$, with residually ramified inertia is already accounted for in the Hecke algebra localised away from the larger set $S$, i.e. that $\pi(U_q)$ lies in the image of $\Psi$; it is one of the generator-by-generator steps in showing that the comparison map between the two localised Hecke algebras is surjective. It is cited by [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.bijective_of_subset_of_charpoly_frobenius_eq`](thm.html#CuspForm.heckeLocal.bijective_of_subset_of_charpoly_frobenius_eq) and by the companion statement for the operators $T_\ell$.
